what we want to see from the chancellor  on wednesday  is an absolute determination to protect  jobs  and businesses in the uk and to secure  our recovery  that must be at the front of the  chancellor's mind  not any party political considerations  about what's best  for the conservative party at the next  general election but he needs to focus  on what's right for our country right  now to secure that recovery  there are lots of reports that rishi  sedak is going to be increasing  corporation tax and something that you  said that you won't support  why is it then that you don't want to  see a rise in corporation tax now but in  2019 you stood as a shadow treasury  minister on a manifesto calling for  higher corporation tax  well like over the last 10 years the  conservatives have pulled our country  further and further away  from the mainstream on corporation tax  and actually that's not  resulted in additional investment we've  seen investment actually  being rised at a lower level in the uk  compared to many other  countries what the conservatives are  doing now though isn't setting out that  long-term plan to rebalance  our tax system in fact they're kicking  the can down the road when it comes to  business rates reform  what they seem to be focused on doing  particularly the chancellor  is trying to get any tax changes out of  the way  quickly get them out of the way so that  they can then have a general election  when they can cut taxes now instead they  should be focused  all of the time on building jobs and  businesses in our country so we can  secure  that economic recovery you know the uk's  had the worst  economic crisis of any major economy  they should be focused on setting that  right not on party political  considerations  um nice answer the question i actually  asked though was  about whether or not why it was that  labour used to think that higher  corporation tax was a good thing and now  you don't  well with respect i just said that we  would welcome  that longer time debate around  corporation tax i just said  that it has been concerning to see how  the consensus party  has pulled us away from the mainstream  on corporation tax but  why are they focused on this now why are  they focused on  increasing council tax directly for  families right now they're focused on it  because the chancellor has said to his  mps  that he wants to get tax changes out of  the way now  so they can have a clear run through to  the next general election  instead they should be focused on  promoting our economy  securing that recovery yes let's have  that long time discussion about tax  particularly to protect our high street  businesses but let's not pretend that  the conservative party is doing this  right now  because they want to secure our recovery  it seems to be driven by party political  considerations  so if rishi cena does propose higher  corporation tax labor's going to support  that vote and favor over it  if we see a long-term plan for getting  our country to a better place  on corporation tax particularly one  which faces up to that enormous gulf and  taxation for businesses based in brics  compared to  businesses based on the internet of  course we will look carefully at that  but what we don't want to see right now  is what the chancellor  is doing particularly in relation to  council tax the imposition of additional  costs  right now at a point when our country is  experiencing the worst  economic crisis of any major economy  so if there's a long-term plan for  corporation tax you'll look carefully at  that  does that mean you'll vote in favor of  higher corporation tax  well we need to look at how this would  be rolled out we need to make sure that  it would be a plan  that would actually make sense for  british business we've got to be doing  all we can to protect jobs and  businesses  right now but as i mentioned before one  of the really big problems with the uk  tax system is that big gulf  between how businesses based on our high  streets are taxed and their business  rates  are a huge problem the chancellor is  kicking the can down the road around  business rates reform now it's not going  to happen  apparently until the autumn that big  gulf between those businesses  and businesses that are largely based on  the internet where corporation tax  is more relevant of course not just the  rate of corporation tax but those  enormous loopholes  that the conservatives have allowed to  develop over the last 10 years  so we would look at any plan very  carefully but we're saying  very directly to the chancellor now  please don't be following  party politics for this budget follow  what is in the interest of our country  and its economic recovery  you mentioned now about the high streets  and the gulf between  online retailers and high street  retailers during the pandemic  does that mean you would support any  taxes aimed at online companies  well we've seen a lot of kite flying  from the government around this  issue and we haven't seen yet any  concrete proposals their digital  services tax really isn't the kind of  robust  change that we need to see to level that  playing field  so we would look at any proposals very  carefully but it's so  important that they do start to level  that playing field we've got businesses  really suffering on the high street  the government's planning changes are  actually going to make that worse i was  astonished to hear  the chancellor say that they would  improve the situation they could lead to  a wholesale  sell-off of our high streets so we  really do need to be getting this right  for the future  um your predecessor john mcdonnell uh  today that told the show last week  he'd like to see a windfall tax on  companies who have profited  um in the from the pandemic banks  property developers  tech companies would you support winfor  tax  well actually i'm sorry to sound like a  stuck record but  i believe what the government should be  focused on doing  right now is on protecting jobs and  businesses  of course there can and should be a  discussion into the future about the  nature of taxation but you know we have  suffered  the worst economic crisis of any major  economy in the uk and we're unique in  another way that we're the only country  that right now has a debate focused on  increasing  tax particularly on families that's the  wrong thing to be doing  right now absolutely let's have that  discussion for the future about taxation  but  right now let's please have a budget  that's focused on jobs  and businesses um you talk a lot about  your debate and discussion  in the future i'm just trying to get a  sense of what you  would do right now are there any tax  risers in this budget that you would  support  well with respect what i would be doing  right now if i were  chancellor is being focused on securing  the recovery what he should have done in  fact not right now but many many  tax rises it should have been clear  about that  sorry he should have been clear about  the continuation  of business rates about the continuation  of the vat reduction he should have  a clear roadmap for the future of wage  support and reform  that system because you know we can have  the chancellor  talking about how he would tax different  forms of economic activity  but if the tax base is shrinking as it  is right now  then that discussion is really a one  remove  from securing our recovery that is what  he should be focused on  um so you seem to be saying that right  now should be focused on on  on spending on supporting and people  during the pandemic are you worried at  all about the state of the public  finances so  clearly the chancellor has got his eye  on that are you worried about debt  levels  well of course they're a concern right  now we do have  very low interest rates but it's not  necessarily the case that that would  continue  forever so we do need to have a sensible  approach to this but  you know we've got to focus on that size  of the tax base you know  how many people are in work how many  businesses are operating  right now we've seen that tax base  shrinking we've seen record  redundancies and so it's really  important that we build that economic  activity back up that's what's happening  in other countries  and really the uk is well out of the  mainstream now  with the chancellor's approach so you  don't seem to support tax rises  you also seem to want more spending and  you're worried about the public finances  it sounds a bit like cake and eat it  boris johnson well no that's that's not  right because we want to see a much more  targeted approach first of all  we've seen billions of pounds wasted by  the government during this crisis the  chancellor  at no point has said that he would crack  down on that waste  so it's really important that we do  tackle it but secondly  we also need to see a much more targeted  approach from government if you look at  the business rates holiday for example  in england that's been provided to every  retailer including the very biggest  essential retailers that's been giving  them support that many  simply did not need in labor-run wales  they've had a much more targeted  approach that's ensured that actually  support has got  to where it's needed so let's talk about  having a targeted approach  let's stamp out that waste and  mismanagement and if we did that then  this crisis wouldn't be proving  as costly as it is under the  conservatives  okay just finally asked rishi sunak at  the end of the interview about shimon  begum the batman green school girl who  of course  left the uk to join islamic state um  do you think it's right that she hasn't  been allowed back to the uk to contest  that decision to strip her  of her british citizenship  well we've had that supreme court  judgment of course it was a complicated  judgment but we respect that judgment we  respect the rule of law

